        "root_concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "section": "urban-planning",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Urban Economics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "```\nMARSHALL'S THREE MECHANISMS:"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md#011",
      "path": [
        "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:urban-planning:06-urban-economics, git-history:urban-planning:06-urban-economics]",
        "canonical_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "current_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:urban-planning:urban-economics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "urban-planning",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "section": "urban-planning",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Urban Economics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "1. LABOR MARKET POOLING (Thick Markets)\n   +-----------------------------------------+\n   |                                         |\n   |  ISOLATED LOCATION:                     |\n   |  Firm A needs 50 engineers.             |\n   |  If engineer quits: hard to replace.    |\n   |  Engineer: If firm closes: hard to      |\n   |  find next job nearby.                  |\n   |  Both face risk of mismatch.            |\n   |                                         |\n   |  CLUSTER:                               |\n   |  100 firms, 5,000 engineers.            |\n   |  Firm A can poach from Firms B-C.       |\n   |  Engineer can move to 99 other firms.   |\n   |  Matching quality improves.             |\n   |  Both sides reduce risk.                |\n   |  Wages higher (competition for labor).  |\n   |  Productivity higher (better matches).  |\n   +-----------------------------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md#012",
      "path": [
        "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:urban-planning:06-urban-economics, git-history:urban-planning:06-urban-economics]",
        "canonical_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "current_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:urban-planning:urban-economics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "urban-planning",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "section": "urban-planning",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Urban Economics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "2. INPUT SHARING (Specialized Suppliers)\n   +-----------------------------------------+\n   |                                         |\n   |  ISOLATED LOCATION:                     |\n   |  Firm A needs specialized legal advice  |\n   |  on semiconductor patents.              |\n   |  Law firm: too small a market to        |\n   |  specialize in just that.               |\n   |                                         |\n   |  CLUSTER:                               |\n   |  100 semiconductor firms.               |\n   |  Enough demand for a specialized law    |\n   |  firm, specialized equipment repair,    |\n   |  specialized recruiting firm.           |\n   |  Division of labor at the cluster level.|\n   |  Each firm gets better specialized      |\n   |  inputs at lower cost.                  |\n   +-----------------------------------------+"
    },
    {
      "id": "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md#013",
      "path": [
        "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:urban-planning:06-urban-economics, git-history:urban-planning:06-urban-economics]",
        "canonical_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "current_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:urban-planning:urban-economics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "urban-planning",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "section": "urban-planning",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Urban Economics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "Agglomeration economies are **platform network effects** applied to physical space: value scales superlinearly with density (Metcalfe's law), creating self-reinforcing feedback loops. Zipf's law for city sizes (rank x size = constant) also appears in network degree distributions and word frequency in information theory."
    },
    {
      "id": "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md#014",
      "path": [
        "06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md"
      ],
      "level": 0,
      "line": 1,
      "metadata": {
        "backsource_ids": "[mdloom-backfill:urban-planning:06-urban-economics, git-history:urban-planning:06-urban-economics]",
        "canonical_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "current_path": "urban-planning/06-URBAN-ECONOMICS.md",
        "id": "maxim:urban-planning:urban-economics",
        "index_roles": "[guide, root-concept]",
        "kind": "guide",
        "maxim_schema": "maxim.frontmatter.v1",
        "module": "urban-planning",
        "remap_from": "[]",
        "remap_to": "[]",
        "root_concepts": "[urban, economics]",
        "section": "urban-planning",
        "source_custody": "partial",
        "status": "source-custody",
        "title": "Urban Economics",
        "updated": "null"
      },
      "text": "3. KNOWLEDGE SPILLOVERS (Face-to-Face)\n   +-----------------------------------------+\n   |                                         |\n   |  Tacit knowledge: cannot be fully       |\n   |  codified in a document or patent.      |\n   |  Transmitted by: observation, informal  |\n   |  conversation, job-hopping, shared      |\n   |  technical communities.                 |\n   |                                         |\n   |  Silicon Valley: engineers job-hop      |\n   |  between firms carrying ideas with      |\n   |  them. AnnaLee Saxenian's \"Regional     |\n   |  Advantage\": contrast with Boston's     |\n   |  Route 128 (proprietary culture,        |\n   |  lower mobility, less innovation).      |\n   |                                         |\n   |  Glaeser: Cities are \"idea machines.\"   |\n   |  Face-to-face interaction produces      |\n   |  innovation that remote interaction     |\n   |  does not replicate.                    |\n   |                                         |\n   |  Zucker/Darby (1996): Star scientists   |\n   |  (highly cited) co-located with biotech |\n   |  startups drove those firms' success.   |\n   |  Geographic proximity to specific       |\n   |  individuals mattered.                  |\n   +-----------------------------------------+\n```"
    },
